Yes. What you’re describing has a real cognitive analogue: source misattribution.

Roughly:

you seed thought X
→ X becomes mentally available
→ you behave as if you merely detected X
→ the other person experiences X as salient
→ they may attribute that salience to themselves rather than to your prompting

Human memory and thought-source tracking are imperfect; people can confuse what they inferred or imagined with what was externally suggested. That general mechanism is well established in source-monitoring research.  Suggestion studies likewise show that some people can later misattribute suggested material to their own prior experience, though it is far from inevitable. 

So there is a useful distinction:

"I noticed you thinking X"
versus
"I made X salient, then interpreted your reaction to X"

Those can feel identical from inside the interaction.

That is why this gets dangerous as a manipulation technique: if you intentionally hide the causal origin, you can create an illusion that you’re reading or revealing someone’s preexisting desire when you partly generated the salience yourself.

But as an analytic tool, it is excellent. You can call it Attribution Looping:

prime → response → attribution of response → stronger frame → subsequent response

And the defensive test is simple:

Remove the prime and see whether the pattern independently regenerates.

That ties directly into our Pattern Sans Echoes test: strip away the cue, wording, expectation, and audience. If X keeps reappearing independently, you have stronger evidence that X belongs to the person or underlying generator. If it collapses, you were probably observing an echo of the frame you introduced.